<b>Interview 30: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;（&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;）： &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;, China Academy of Art teacher, male, born in the 1960s, from Hangzhou in Zhejiang</b>

<p><b>
How do you think Chinese and Western art differs?</b>

<br>
There's no big difference to speak of. If anything is different it's the system.

<p>Western contemporary art started earlier, the process is more mature, the foundations are more solid, from a wider perspective, the masses have a higher understanding of art. In China, I feel it's the opposite, it's even better, more vivid and lively. Contemporary art grows best in this environment, like weeds growing chaotically. If it's too clean, "water that is too clear attracts few fish," there's no excitement, there's no controversy. The Chinese art world undoubtedly wants even more thrills and challenges and less uncertainty. China's artists all somewhat favor youth. I've become aware of my aging. Young people can't do elderly things, but the elderly also can't do young people things. This small city of Kassel is exactly how I had imagined Europe. Very peaceful, nothing unexpectedly makes you boil with anger, this is the best place to live. This kind of life provides for the elderly, there's no struggle, it's possible to harm and be harmed, but it's not enough to upset or provoke.

<p><b>
What do you think of "Fairytale" as an artwork?</b>

<br>I've read many reviews but none of them explain the piece's significance, they evade addressing the meaning. I think they do this intentionally, not out of stupidity or feigned naivety. It's irrelevant if an artwork raises the question of whether it counts as art, this definition isn't something an artist can determine, it has a context. The entire context produces this type of work, everyone can evaluate it, no one person is an authority on the subject, not event the artist. If the artist is too quick to assess, he or she is in fact limiting his or her own work, narrowing the road of interpretation. In reality, to some extent, anything can give rise to controversy, for example regarding the definition of contemporary art, its boundaries, whether it negates traditional art, or replaces it, or whether it lead to the establishment, creation and development of new art. Critics and audiences discuss this together, this embodies art's lively nature. The harder it is to delimit and the more unpredictable it is, the more fascinating it is.

<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'s piece is especially timely, I think it's extremely meaningful, there's no need to assess it positively or negatively, I rely on intuition. I participated in this piece, I thought it was very fun and thought provoking. Even though &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; explains it very well, I don't necessarily want to know, I might have my own interpretation.

<p><b>There are some young people who don't really think, is it because they're too young and aren't yet aware of their strength, or is it because Chinese society made them unable to think?</b>

<br>They still haven't had the time to figure out how to exist and survive due to China's education system and societal context. In the West, young people can start thinking about their future early on, they can reflect on how to cultivate themselves, whereas the Chinese worry about what to do if a child's grades aren't good. These young people play games with a fervor all night long, at first I couldn't understand why, then I thought they may be seeking out another identity to survive. Today's system neither provides them with means for thought nor encourages them to think.

<p><b>Do you think there are political or ideological connotations to                   's act of bringing over so many Ming and Qing dynasty chairs?</b>

<br>This kind of performance certainly has a political aspect to it, but doesn't necessarily play a major part, it might be incidental, it might be unconscious or involuntary, &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'s background and the materials he uses have a political tint to them. In Chinese culture, chairs themselves indicate rank, status and power. I truly think his principal concern isn't political, it's in raising a question with multiple divers answers. Chinese and Europeans may have completely different understandings of the chairs, this give the piece lasting power, making it more than just a flash in the pan. This is something many people can discuss, but there is no conclusion to the matter, just like "Fairytale." 1001 chairs isn't an assembly of each individual case, it's a collective bombardment, it's very bold, at first it astounds you, it puts you in a relatively low position to think it over. If it wasn't 1001 people, if it was just a few, it would also be a performance, it would also be an event, but it may not attract any attention. Using quantity is even more weighty than using chairs as props, if it were 1001 rocks or something else instead, it would also work. The chairs' symbolism carry other implications, but their power in numbers is even greater than their symbolism. If it makes people think, then you've succeeded. There is no way to define what he has put forth, it's not a mature work, but this 'immature-ness' was premeditated.

<p><b>What's your opinion of Fairytale?</b>

<br>I quite like it. Let's not forget, I'm here for free to have fun. If I was an outsider looking at this project, I would also consider it extremely interesting. No one has ever dared to do something like this. When I saw                        &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'s blog, I recommended it to several friends, they all laughed and thought, "how could this be?" As it happened, the event was unfortunately planned for April 1st. I said, "this is a good thing, I like it, I'm signing up, even if it's an April fool's trick I'll still be happy." No one's ever thought of or dare to think or something of this scale, let alone successfully accomplished it, the meaning behind it is already unimportant. No delimitation is meaningful, that's the critics' problem. If you are contemporary art's hot new thing, everyone is struck dumb, some people say you're great, some people say you're terrible, I reckon they're as many people who think your work good as there are who think it's bad. This has to do with good versus evil, I think old                isn't trying to write essays on the issue, if you say you uphold 'good', then you eliminate all other possibilities. He might not care if it is or isn't art, the most important thing is for everyone to start thinking.

<p><b>What problems do you find in the Chinese art world?</b>

<br>The awareness that Westerners lead and manage it still persists. All the good galleries and good exhibitions are Western, there are some Eastern ones, but they're considered unexpected sparks, there's also some official government ones, but there's no way to enter or gain their recognition. China will slowly take shape and become itself, not simply a word-for-word copy, it also won't build itself along the lines of what other people say it the way to go.

<p><b>Do you think Chinese contemporary art is related to our current economic development and system of values?</b>

<br>Certainly.  Everything is planted in this soil, only the voices differ, with other possibilities. It doesn't display political tendencies, even though they exist nowadays, but these elements are really quite rare. It's good that society has developed quite reasonably, it just needs some sensibility and intuition. Art surpasses reason to express thoughts that free and loosen boundaries, you can't restrict it. Some people say art is chaotic, I agree, but I commend that as a positive corollary. 

